# Code owners (#673 Phase 2).
#
# One maintainer today, so every line below says the same thing — which makes this file
# look pointless. It is not: it is the **shape** the repository is meant to grow into,
# written while the directories are still clean enough to draw it. Each block is a real
# ownership boundary already enforced by the crate graph, so adding a second maintainer
# for the renderer or for Mind is a one-line edit rather than a reorganisation.
#
# GitHub applies the LAST matching pattern, so the specific blocks below the catch-all
# are what actually take effect for those paths.

*                           @james-andrew-walsh

# The engine spine. Changes here reach all three products, and `ipc.rs` is the one file
# whose byte layout is load-bearing across a process boundary.
/native/organon-core/       @james-andrew-walsh
/native/organon-render/     @james-andrew-walsh

# Per-product front-of-house.
/native/organon-mind/       @james-andrew-walsh
/native/organon-shell/      @james-andrew-walsh

# 🔒 Plugin identity and the parameter chain. `lib.rs` carries the VST3 class ID and the
# CLAP ID; changing either orphans the plugin in every saved DAW session.
/native/src/lib.rs          @james-andrew-walsh
/native/src/params.rs       @james-andrew-walsh
/native/src/param_table.rs  @james-andrew-walsh

# Build, install and CI — the paths that decide what a user actually receives.
/native/bundle.sh           @james-andrew-walsh
/native/bundle.ps1          @james-andrew-walsh
/native/deploy.sh           @james-andrew-walsh
/native/deploy.ps1          @james-andrew-walsh
/native/xtask/              @james-andrew-walsh
/.github/                   @james-andrew-walsh

